  "item_results": [
    {
      "item_id": "ce841aab-a26b-512b-8fa9-ba28cf3b0f20",
      "session_id": "S3-4-who-are-the-top-10-most-important-characters",
      "trace_id": "8c15d2e0eb1f062350f3f1677ade5c28",
      "output": {
        "question": "Who are the top 10 most important characters?",
        "answer": "The books do not contain a ranked list or explicit statement identifying the \"top 10 most important characters.\" Importance is subjective and not enumerated in the text. However, the following characters are central to the narrative across the trilogy:\n\n1. **Lisbeth Salander**: The protagonist, described as \"the most competent person\" [E1] and \"the most asocial human being\" [E2].\n2. **Mikael Blomkvist**: The journalist protagonist who investigates the cases alongside Salander [E3].\n3. **Henrik Vanger**: The patriarch who hires Blomkvist to solve the mystery of Harriet's disappearance in *The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo*.\n4. **Zalachenko (Van Liere)**: Lisbeth's father and the primary antagonist in *The Girl Who Played with Fire* and *The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest*, a former Russian agent [E4].\n5. **Martin Vanger**: Henrik Vanger's nephew, who is revealed to be the murderer in the first book.\n6. **Göran Gustafson**: The head of Security Services (Säpo) who hires Blomkvist and later becomes involved in the conspiracy against Salander.\n7. **Hans-Erik Wennerström**: The corrupt billionaire whose crimes are exposed by Blomkvist in the first book.\n8. **Dag Svensson**: The author murdered at the beginning of the first book, whose research leads to the main plot.\n9. **Mihrab Mirzani**: Lisbeth's friend and hacker ally who helps her throughout the series.\n10. **Nils Björman**: The security officer assigned to watch Zalachenko in the past [E5].\n\nOther significant characters include Annika Giannini (Blomkvist's lawyer/sister), Dr. Peter Teleborian (Salander's guardian/antagonist), and Gullberg (the Swedish intelligence officer involved in Zalachenko's defection) [E6].",
        "citations": [
          "[E1]",
          "[E2]",
          "[E3]",
          "[E4]",
          "[E5]",
          "[E6]"
        ],
        "reranked": [
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c23_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 123,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 23 Friday, July 1–Sunday, July 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "“Thank you for agreeing to come in,” Ekström said. “I have read this . . . hmm . . . account that arrived this morning, and there are a few matters I’d like to clarify.”\n“I’ll do what I can to help,” Giannini said.\n“I don’t know exactly where to start. Let me say from the outset that both Inspector Faste and I are profoundly astonished.”\n“Indeed?”\n“I’m trying to understand what your objective is.”",
            "paragraph_end": 127,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "000c56f1ae41b1ba8c889a422eb93d6aa822b3377c6f157e73298aea69927f0f",
            "id": "000c56f1-ae41-b1ba-8c88-9a422eb93d6a",
            "agent_label": "E1"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c03_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 182,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 3 Friday, April 8–Saturday, April 9",
            "page": null,
            "text": "He wrote an email address on a page torn from his notebook.\n“This is an untraceable Hotmail address. You can use it if you have anything to tell me. Don’t use your official address, obviously; just set up your own temporary Hotmail account.”\nShe put the address in the inside pocket of her jacket. She did not make him any promises.\nInspector Erlander woke at 7:00 on Saturday morning to the ringing of his phone. He heard voices from the TV and smelled coffee from the kitchen, where his wife was already doing her morning chores. He had returned to his apartment in Mölndal at 1:00 in the morning, having been on duty for twenty-two hours, so he was far from wide awake when he reached to answer it.",
            "paragraph_end": 185,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "0061181d1b8ce3b08d6635c831876f26b0e2912ee1412fb16e898d6073771c72",
            "id": "0061181d-1b8c-e3b0-8d66-35c831876f26",
            "agent_label": "E2"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo",
            "book_file": "The Girl with the Dragon Tattoo -- Larsson, Stieg -- Millennium book, 1st Vintage Crime_Black Lizard mass-market -- Random House, Incorporated -- isbn13 9780307455352 -- f10e68d0e06407c85b6ba58f6f49d618 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "The_Girl_with_the_Dragon_Tattoo_split_037.html",
            "paragraph": 160,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 27 Saturday, July 26– Monday, July 28",
            "page": "36",
            "text": "“He also has a degree in economics from Oxford and a law degree from Melbourne.”\nBlomkvist thought about the sweaty, muscular man with his shirt off who had driven him into and through the ravine; he tried to imagine him in a pinstripe suit. Why not?\n“All of this will take time to work out,” Frode said. “But she would be a perfect CEO. With the right support team she could represent a whole new deal for the company.”",
            "paragraph_end": 162,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "00382b27f3229234ec365c5c1af1c784137d8900d419be579d90881085171450",
            "id": "00382b27-f322-9234-ec36-5c5c1af1c784",
            "agent_label": "E3"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 126,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "By that time it was already too late. Zalachenko had gone and fucked that stupid whore Agneta Sofia Salander, née Sjölander, and he had heedlessly told her his real name—Zalachenko. Gullberg began to believe that Zalachenko was not quite right in the head. He suspected that the Russian defector wanted to be exposed. It was as if he needed a platform. How else to explain the fact that he had been so fucking stupid?",
            "paragraph_end": 126,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "a22f4f5ba0e175c40ebcdfc0cf51e98c610c170267d6b6c2e18be06aa75b135b",
            "id": "a22f4f5b-a0e1-75c4-0ebc-dfc0cf51e98c",
            "agent_label": "E4"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 127,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "There were whores, there were periods of excessive drinking, and there were incidents of violence with bouncers and others. On three occasions Zalachenko was arrested by the Swedish police for drunkenness, and twice more in connection with fights in bars. Every time, the Section had to intervene discreetly and bail him out, seeing to it that documents disappeared and records were altered. Gullberg assigned Björck to babysit the defector almost around the clock. It was not an easy job, but there was no alternative.",
            "paragraph_end": 127,
            "part_end": 1,
            "embedding_model": "sentence-transformers/multi-qa-MiniLM-L6-cos-v1",
            "source_id": "9a06654c0052d55df0a1aceda750b5aad12c0265b7aa9b96dec5ae1522528356",
            "id": "9a06654c-0052-d55d-f0a1-aceda750b5aa",
            "agent_label": "E5"
          },
          {
            "book": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest",
            "book_file": "The Girl Who Kicked the Hornet's Nest -- Stieg Larsson -- Millennium Trilogy #3, 2010 -- Random House, Inc_ -- a9a1c0e6f4b8fd47fa0771ff9463bc34 -- Anna’s Archive.epub",
            "section": "OEBPS/Lars_9780307593672_epub_c05_r1.htm",
            "paragraph": 121,
            "part": 1,
            "chapter": "CHAPTER 5 Sunday, April 10",
            "page": null,
            "text": "The hopes for a breakthrough, however, were soon dashed, since Zalachenko had never operated in Sweden and had little knowledge of the country. On the other hand, Zalachenko had heard the rumour of a “Red Jumper,” a highly placed Swede—or possibly other Scandinavian politician—who worked for the KGB.\nGullberg drew up a list of names that were connected to Palme: Carl Lidbom, Pierre Schori, Sten Andersson, Marita Ulvskog, and a number of others. For the rest of his life, Gullberg would come back again and again to that list, but he never found an answer.",
            "paragraph_end": 122,
            "part_end": 1,
